There's no existing project in the directory. Since you mentioned you have an existing project, here's exactly what to add:

## 1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Default handler returns 500 with no sensitive details

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

## 2. Wire it up in `Program.cs`

```csharp
// Add these services (before builder.Build())
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

// Add this middleware (after app is built, before app.MapXxx())
app.UseExceptionHandler();
app.UseStatusCodePages();
```

## How it works

- **Known exceptions** (`KeyNotFoundException`, `ArgumentException`, `InvalidOperationException`) are caught by your `IExceptionHandler` and mapped to the appropriate status code with a safe Problem Details body.
- **Unhandled exceptions** fall through (`return false`), and the default `UseExceptionHandler` + `AddProblemDetails()` combination returns a generic 500 response in RFC 7807 format with no sensitive details leaked.
- **No try-catch needed** in individual endpoints — the middleware handles everything globally.

All error responses will look like:

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Not Found",
  "instance": "/api/products/999"
}
```
